Chapter: ModBoundReturn

 A bounded return whose value is a remainder by a positive literal.

 `int-mod x n` is bounded by [0, n - 1]. The emit-side prover always knew
 that; the checker did not, because it resolved builtins through a table
 of CONSTANT intervals and this bound depends on an argument. So these
 three functions were REFUSED with CDX2051 quoting the full i64 interval
 -- a provable bounded return that could not be written without __narrow,
 which reintroduces the runtime trap the bound exists to discharge.

 Compiling at all is the test. Each function is also printed, so a range
 derived wrongly in the other direction changes an answer rather than
 passing quietly.

Section: Probe

  ret-direct : Integer -> Integer between 0 and 255
  ret-direct (n) = int-mod n 200

  ret-via-let : Integer -> Integer between 0 and 255
  ret-via-let (n) =
   let v = int-mod n 100
   in v

  ret-tighter : Integer -> Integer between 0 and 9
  ret-tighter (n) = int-mod n 10

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni (show (ret-direct 1234))
   print-line-uni (show (ret-via-let 1234))
   print-line-uni (show (ret-tighter 1234))
  end
